# Order Get It Right -- .gitattributes
#
# Cross-platform line-ending normalisation. Windows checkouts
# historically produced noisy diffs on every Markdown and Python
# file (CRLF vs LF). This file pins the policy so every clone,
# every contributor, every CI run produces the same bytes.
#
# - Text files default to LF in the repo, CRLF on Windows checkout.
#   This matches the historical Windows convention for Python
#   source and Markdown while keeping the repo portable.
# - Binary files (images, PDFs, compiled artefacts) are marked
#   binary so Git never tries to normalise them.
#
# F11 (Git adoption), 2026-07-18.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Python: LF in the repo, CRLF on Windows checkout. The runtime
# doesn't care; the tests don't care. This is purely to keep
# diffs clean.
*.py            text eol=lf
*.pyi           text eol=lf

# Markdown / docs: LF in repo, CRLF on Windows.
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.rst           text eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch scripts: CRLF on disk and in repo. Windows
# is the only target for these; CRLF in the repo means no
# checkout-time rewrite, no false-positive diffs.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# C / C++ / Rust: LF in the repo (the toolchain expects LF).
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.cpp           text eol=lf
*.hpp           text eol=lf
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf

# JSON: the chain files must hash byte-identically across hosts.
# The chain serialiser writes LF-only JSON, so we pin LF in repo
# AND on checkout. A CRLF rewrite of a chain file would change
# the file SHA-256 -- catastrophic for tamper evidence.
*.json          text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.msi           binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.pyd           binary
*.pyc           binary
*.pyo           binary